The model, verified:

```css
:root {
  --space-1: 0.5rem;
  --brand: hsl(220 60% 50%);
  --surface: hsl(220 20% 98%);
  --text: hsl(220 30% 15%);
}

[data-theme='dark'] {              /* override at ANY subtree scope */
  --surface: hsl(220 20% 10%);
  --text: hsl(220 20% 95%);
}

.card {
  padding: var(--space-1);
  background: var(--surface);
  color: var(--text, black);         /* fallback chain — resolve left to right */
}

/* the fallback is a FULL expression, not just a value: */
border: 1px solid var(--border-color, hsl(0 0% 80% / 0.5));
```

The cascade + inheritance pair (the whole power): a custom property is an ordinary cascading property (later rules, higher specificity win) AND it inherits — so a card set on a wrapper overrides :root for the subtree. That's the theming architecture in one sentence: define at :root, override at [data-theme] or a scoped wrapper, consume with var() everywhere — the SAME components render any theme by reassigning ~10 tokens.

The JS channel (the runtime half): custom properties are WRITABLE from JS — element.style.setProperty('--x', v) — and READABLE via getComputedStyle(el).getPropertyValue('--x'). That's the modern contract for JS state → CSS: JS writes the token, CSS consumes it in transforms, gradients, animations — no style-object surgery, no per-property JS animation loops. (el.style.getProperty/setProperty read/write the INLINE declaration only — the computed form needs getComputedStyle: the distinction interviewers check.)

The runtime vs compile-time split (vs Sass/Less variables): preprocessor variables are substituted AWAY at build time — no runtime theming, no media-query reassignment, no JS access. Custom properties are LIVE at runtime — everything above is impossible with $var. The cost: var() resolves at use time, so invalid-at-computed-value-time falls back to the property's INITIAL value (not the previous declaration — the guaranteed-invalid behavior worth one sentence).
